    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_26537698b69b5c5885b15318ed618e9b331e11cd1560b9ea4a9a2ae0d1354d59",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The period integral packages the critical modular-form L-values as coefficients of one polynomial. Applying the completed L-function's functional equation to those coefficients gives a reciprocal relation and hence an invariant circle for the polynomial's root pairing. A half-polynomial removes the redundant reciprocal half; on the unit circle its reflected sum becomes a real sine or cosine polynomial, so proving the required number of sign changes, together with degree and multiplicity accounting, proves that every period-polynomial root lies on the circle.",
