    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_8102957b8ec7eee691b434cd0c122156f89c4ca98e2c47e81e7136ea03eb9d6c",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "After normalizing P_f to Q_f, the large-weight argument separates an explicit exponential model from three errors. On the unit circle the model's phase must be monotone so that its alternating extrema enumerate the required sign intervals; independently, the total error must be strictly smaller than the model's minimum amplitude so those signs survive for Q_f. Explicit bounds on coefficient error, exponential-tail error, and the central term turn this into weight-level thresholds. A later, sharper uniform approximation and two-sided tests around each model phase zero then trap one true root nearby, upgrading the global circle count to asymptotic localization.",
