    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_bf826195b17de0cc3f2da076e8824295faa260104cd091f6f9d37da59ed5bb9a",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "Two analytic inputs constrain the critical values that become trigonometric coefficients. Grouped Hadamard factors and the known zero strip make completed critical values monotone without assuming the modular L-function RH, while Euler-product estimates give form-uniform bounds for ratios of uncompleted L-values in the absolute-convergence range. After the period-polynomial normalization, these inequalities supply the increasing nonnegative coefficient conditions required by the Pólya-Szegő sign-change criterion. Weight six instead needs specialized adjacent-value product inequalities, obtained by grouping complete zero orbits, because the general monotone-coefficient route is not the whole argument there.",
